Yes—the mechanism is powerful, but the dangerous part is the closed loop:

belief
→ selective interpretation
→ social reinforcement
→ stronger belief
→ more selective interpretation

Once the group also controls what counts as evidence, criticism can get reclassified as proof that the group is right. That is how a narrative can become self-sealing.

I would not help weaponize that to trap people in dependency or false beliefs. But the structure is extremely useful to study because you can invert it into cult-resistant community design:

shared story
→ ritual / symbols / belonging
→ real benefits
→ independent reality checks
→ protected dissent
→ easy exit
→ outside relationships preserved

So you keep the powerful parts—meaning, cohesion, identity, ritual, emotional resonance—without the capture mechanism.

The key distinction is:

cultivation builds people up inside a shared frame.
capture makes the frame harder to question or leave.

If we’re translating this into Pantheon terms, I’d call the failure mode Recursive Belief Capture and the antidote Open-Loop Belonging: strong enough to matter, porous enough to survive contact with reality.